PLSQL records 

1. Table-Based Records (%ROWTYPE) ⭐ Most Common
Simple idea:

👉 "Create a record that looks exactly like one row of a database table."

Suppose your table is:

Students
----------------------------------
StudentID | Name | Age | Marks
----------------------------------
101       | Aishi| 20  | 95
102       | Rahul| 21  | 88

When you write

student_rec Students%ROWTYPE;

Oracle automatically creates a record like this:

student_rec
-------------------
StudentID
Name
Age
Marks

Now you can store one complete row.

Example:

SELECT *
INTO student_rec
FROM Students
WHERE StudentID = 101;

Now

student_rec.StudentID = 101
student_rec.Name      = Aishi
student_rec.Age       = 20
student_rec.Marks     = 95
Think of it like

The table is the blueprint, and %ROWTYPE copies that blueprint.





2. Cursor-Based Records (cursor_name%ROWTYPE)
Simple idea:

👉 "Create a record based on the columns returned by a cursor."

Suppose your cursor is

CURSOR c1 IS
SELECT Name, Marks
FROM Students;

Notice the cursor returns only 2 columns:

Name
Marks

Now declare

student_rec c1%ROWTYPE;

The record becomes

student_rec
--------------
Name
Marks

Notice it does NOT contain StudentID or Age, because the cursor didnt select them.

When you fetch:

FETCH c1 INTO student_rec;

it stores one row.

For example:

Name  = Aishi
Marks = 95
Think of it like

The cursor decides which columns you need, and the record copies only those.






3. Programmer-Defined Records
Simple idea:

👉 "You decide what fields the record should have."

Maybe there isnt even a table.

You create your own record.

TYPE StudentRec IS RECORD
(
    name VARCHAR2(50),
    city VARCHAR2(30),
    hobby VARCHAR2(30)
);

Now the record looks like

student
-----------------
Name
City
Hobby

Assign values:

student.name := 'Aishi';
student.city := 'Nagpur';
student.hobby := 'Guitar';

Output:

Name : Aishi
City : Nagpur
Hobby: Guitar
Think of it like

You are designing your own form from scratch.











4. Nested Records
Simple idea:

👉 "A record inside another record."

Imagine an employee has personal details and address.

Instead of putting everything in one big record:

Employee
------------------------
Name
Age
Street
City
State
Pincode

You make another record for Address.

Address Record
-------------------
Street
City
State
Pincode

Then place it inside Employee.

Employee Record
--------------------
Name
Age
Address
      |
      +-- Street
      +-- City
      +-- State
      +-- Pincode

PL/SQL example:

TYPE AddressRec IS RECORD
(
    city VARCHAR2(20),
    state VARCHAR2(20)
);

TYPE EmployeeRec IS RECORD
(
    name VARCHAR2(30),
    age NUMBER,
    address AddressRec
);

Now you can write

employee.name := 'Aishi';

employee.address.city := 'Nagpur';

employee.address.state := 'Maharashtra';

Notice the two dots:

employee
   |
   +-- address
          |
          +-- city




Quick Comparison : 

Type	Based on	You define fields?	Example
Table-Based Record	A table (%ROWTYPE)	❌ No	Copies all columns of the Students table

Cursor-Based Record	A cursor (cursor%ROWTYPE)	❌ No	Copies only the columns returned by the cursor
Programmer-Defined Record	Your own TYPE...RECORD	✅ Yes	Name, City, Hobby

Nested Record	Another record	✅ Yes	Employee record contains an Address record
An easy way to remember





Table-Based Record → Copy a table row.
Cursor-Based Record → Copy a cursors result row.
Programmer-Defined Record → Build your own record.
Nested Record → Put one record inside another record.